INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A dye house''s process control system monitors dye bath TEMPERATURE RAMP RATE (degrees per minute during heating) as a critical parameter, not just final target temperature. For reactive dyeing specifically, why can too-FAST a ramp rate cause quality problems even if the FINAL temperature and total time at that temperature are both correct?', '["Ramp rate has no relationship to dyeing quality as long as the final temperature is eventually correct, making this entire process-control parameter technically unnecessary to monitor","A too-fast temperature ramp can cause the dye to exhaust onto the fiber UNEVENLY before the bath and fabric have reached uniform temperature throughout (different parts of the liquor/fabric mass heating at slightly different rates), locking in shade unevenness before the system has had time to equilibrate — a controlled, gradual ramp rate allows more uniform heat distribution and more even, controlled exhaustion kinetics throughout the entire bath/fabric mass, directly connecting to the earlier premature-alkali-addition and dye-migration discussions about timing-sensitive dyeing uniformity","Ramp rate only affects the TOTAL processing time, with zero relationship to final shade evenness or quality outcomes","A faster ramp rate always IMPROVES shade evenness compared to a slower, more controlled ramp rate, the opposite of the actual relationship between heating rate and dyeing uniformity"]'::jsonb, 'A too-fast temperature ramp can cause the dye to exhaust onto the fiber UNEVENLY before the bath and fabric have reached uniform temperature throughout (different parts of the liquor/fabric mass heating at slightly different rates), locking in shade unevenness before the system has had time to equilibrate — a controlled, gradual ramp rate allows more uniform heat distribution and more even, controlled exhaustion kinetics throughout the entire bath/fabric mass, directly connecting to the earlier premature-alkali-addition and dye-migration discussions about timing-sensitive dyeing uniformity', 'This connects the earlier reactive-dye-sequencing/timing-sensitivity and dye-migration discussions to a specific, additional process-control parameter — since dye exhaustion kinetics are temperature-dependent, and a large volume of liquor/fabric doesn''t heat perfectly uniformly everywhere simultaneously during a FAST temperature ramp (some areas/points genuinely reach the target temperature before others), a too-rapid ramp can cause different parts of the bath/fabric to exhaust dye at different rates during this non-equilibrium heating period, locking in shade unevenness before the whole system has had time to reach uniform temperature — a controlled, gradual ramp rate gives the system time to equilibrate more evenly throughout, directly paralleling why timing/rate control (not just final target values) matters throughout dyeing process control.', 3, '{"temperature-ramp-rate-uniformity"}'::text[]),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A finishing plant applies a durable press resin finish and measures ''formaldehyde release'' (a regulated byproduct/residual from some cross-linking chemistries, connecting to the earlier azo-dye-compliance discussion pattern) using a specific test method with results expressed in ppm. If a buyer''s limit is 75ppm (a common infant-wear-adjacent limit) but the standard adult-apparel limit for the SAME market is 300ppm, why would a technical team need to know the GARMENT''S END-USE CATEGORY before evaluating a single test result against ''the'' limit?', '["Formaldehyde limits are always identical regardless of end-use category, making this described category-specific limit variation technically incorrect","Regulatory/buyer formaldehyde limits are typically TIERED by end-use category specifically because different user populations carry different vulnerability/exposure risk profiles (e.g., infants/young children generally warrant stricter limits than general adult apparel, similar in underlying risk-based logic to the earlier flammability-regulation discussion targeting children''s sleepwear) — a single test result (e.g., 120ppm) could PASS against the adult-apparel limit (300ppm) while FAILING against the infant-wear limit (75ppm) for the exact same measured value, meaning correctly evaluating any compliance result requires first knowing which specific end-use category''s limit actually applies to this particular product","A single formaldehyde limit always applies universally to every garment category with zero variation, making this described tiered-limit system technically nonexistent in real regulatory practice","Formaldehyde release testing has no relationship to any regulatory compliance framework, making this entire discussion of category-specific limits technically irrelevant to real finishing quality control"]'::jsonb, 'Regulatory/buyer formaldehyde limits are typically TIERED by end-use category specifically because different user populations carry different vulnerability/exposure risk profiles (e.g., infants/young children generally warrant stricter limits than general adult apparel, similar in underlying risk-based logic to the earlier flammability-regulation discussion targeting children''s sleepwear) — a single test result (e.g., 120ppm) could PASS against the adult-apparel limit (300ppm) while FAILING against the infant-wear limit (75ppm) for the exact same measured value, meaning correctly evaluating any compliance result requires first knowing which specific end-use category''s limit actually applies to this particular product', 'This directly extends the earlier risk-based, population-vulnerability-tiered regulation pattern (already established for flammability standards specifically targeting children''s sleepwear) to formaldehyde compliance — since different end-use categories carry genuinely different user vulnerability profiles (infants/young children generally warranting stricter protective limits than general adult use), formaldehyde limits are similarly TIERED by category rather than universal — this means the SAME numeric test result can be a clear PASS for one product category and a clear FAIL for another, making correct identification of the applicable end-use category limit an essential PREREQUISITE step before any compliance result can be meaningfully evaluated, exactly the same risk-based regulatory logic pattern already established for flammability standards, now recognized as a broader principle across multiple different regulated textile safety parameters.', 3, '{"formaldehyde-tiered-limits"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
